# Poolside Laguna S 2.1 (July 2026, checkpoint updated August), NVFP4: 118B
# with 8B active, built for agentic coding and trained with a long-context
# stage up to 1,048,576 tokens, so nothing is stretched. It reasons between
# tool calls (poolside_v1 parsers) and wants its earlier reasoning kept in the
# history, which fable does. 12 of 48 layers are global attention with 8 KV
# heads of 128; the other 36 use a 512-token sliding window.
#
# The catch on this box is size. The checkpoint is 99.7 GB (93 GiB): only the
# routed experts are 4-bit. The cache is FP8, as the checkpoint was calibrated,
# at 25 KB per token, and what is left after the weights holds roughly 600k to
# 750k tokens. MAX_LEN below is the largest window that fits; a full 1M needs
# a machine with more memory. License: OpenMDW-1.1.
#
# Not measured yet: the weights are on the Spark, and neither the window that
# fits nor recall at it has been checked.
HF_REPO=poolside/Laguna-S-2.1-NVFP4
SERVED_NAME=laguna-s-2.1
MAX_LEN=500000
LONG_MAX_LEN=
ROPE_ORIG=
TOOL_PARSER=poolside_v1
REASONING_PARSER=poolside_v1
KV_DTYPE=auto
# 0.88 of 121.7 GiB is 107 GiB: 93 for weights and about 12 for the cache,
# which is 500k tokens. Poolside's own Spark recipe uses 0.85 and 262144.
MEM_UTIL=0.88
MIN_FREE_GB=105
# From the card's DGX Spark recipe: the arch string for the FP4 kernel JIT and
# a cap on its build fan-out. The first start takes about 15 minutes.
DOCKER_ENV="CUTE_DSL_ARCH=sm_121a MAX_JOBS=4"
# The kernel autotune at start-up wants memory this profile cannot spare; with
# less headroom than this it ran the GPU driver out of memory on another model.
EXTRA_ARGS='--default-chat-template-kwargs {"enable_thinking":true} --no-enable-flashinfer-autotune'
